  {hreflang}
  lang="en"
  lastmod="2026-10-12"
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-4">
    <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />
  </div>

  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <p class="hero-tag">Divorce Guide · Sharjah 2026</p>
      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="hero-desc">
        Divorcing in Sharjah follows the UAE’s federal personal status laws, the same ones used in Dubai and Abu Dhabi, but the courts now belong to Sharjah’s own judiciary and there is no English-language civil family court. This guide covers where the Sharjah family court is, its hours, how cases are handled, the step-by-step process, documents and costs, and how Sharjah differs from Dubai and Abu Dhabi.
      </p>
      <p class="hero-author">By <strong>Aleksi Suon</strong> · UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated October 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="page-layout">
    <main class="main-content">

      <div class="answer-box">
        <h2>Quick summary of divorcing in Sharjah</h2>
        <p>
          Divorce in Sharjah is governed by <strong>UAE federal personal status law</strong>: the Muslim track under <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</strong> and the non-Muslim civil track under <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022</strong>.
          Cases are heard by the <strong>Sharjah Courts</strong>, part of Sharjah’s own judiciary since 2025, in the Al Layyah (Al Khan) area near Khalid Port; listed hours are Monday to Thursday, 7:30 am to 3:30 pm.
          In Muslim cases the judge may refer you to Family Guidance first; it is no longer mandatory, and civil divorces of non-Muslims are not referred.
          Lawyer fees vary widely with complexity (indicative ranges below).
          Appeals must be filed within <strong>30 days</strong> of the judgment.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Sharjah family court: where it is, hours and how cases are handled</h2>
        <p>
          Sharjah family cases are heard by the Sharjah Courts, which since 2025 belong to the emirate's own judiciary rather than the federal court system. The Sharjah Judiciary Platform, the official site of Sharjah's Judicial Council, Judicial Department and Public Prosecution, gives the address as the <strong>Al Layyah (Al Khan) area, near Khalid Port, Sharjah</strong>, and the working hours as <strong>Monday to Thursday, 7:30 am to 3:30 pm</strong>. Individual counters and courts outside Sharjah city can keep different hours, so check the platform or call before you go.
        </p>
        <h3>Who runs the Sharjah courts now</h3>
        <p>
          Sharjah used to sit inside the federal judiciary run by the Ministry of Justice. That changed in stages. An Emiri Decree of 23 October 2024 created the Sharjah Judicial Department, Sharjah's Law No. 7 of 2025 on the Regulation of Judicial Authority took effect in June 2025, and the Sharjah Court of Cassation, now the emirate's highest court, held its first session on 7 July 2025. Some older pages, including the federal government portal's page on the judiciary, still list Sharjah under the federal courts, so do not rely on them for where to file or appeal.
        </p>
        <p>
          In June 2025 the Sharjah Judicial Council decided to set up a Family Court as a specialised court, and approved a fund to pay urgent alimony where it cannot be collected from the person who owes it; the fund later recovers the money from that person. Separately, a court can order <a href="/en/temporary-alimony-uae/">temporary alimony</a> while a maintenance case is heard. In December 2025 the Council approved two Courts of Appeal, in Kalba and Al Dhaid. We have not found an official announcement of the date each of these started work, so ask the court or your lawyer which circuit handles your case.
        </p>
        <h3>Can you file in Sharjah?</h3>
        <p>
          It depends on who files. As a general rule a case goes to the court where the respondent lives or works. A wife, a child, a parent or a custodian bringing a claim for divorce, khula, alimony, custody or visitation can also file where either party lives or works, or where the marital home is (Article 3 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). So a wife living in Sharjah can usually file in Sharjah even if her husband lives in Dubai. Where you married does not decide it.
        </p>
        <h3>How a family case is handled</h3>
        <ul class="doc-list">
          <li><strong>Filing online or in person.</strong> The Sharjah Judiciary Platform (sjd.ae), launched in January 2026, offers digital court services for personal status cases, alongside civil, commercial, labour and execution cases, so many procedures no longer need a visit. Lawyers and typing centres can still file for you.</li>
          <li><strong>The law applied.</strong> Sharjah applies the federal family laws: Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 for Muslims and Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 for non-Muslims. Procedure follows the Civil Procedure Code, Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022, which allows registration, service, hearings and judgments to be done wholly or partly by remote means (Article 330), and any party may ask for a hearing in person (Article 333).</li>
          <li><strong>Family Guidance.</strong> In a Muslim case the supervising judge may refer you to Family Guidance first, but only if a settlement looks feasible (Article 8 of the 2024 law). Urgent and temporary orders on alimony and custody, and cases to prove a divorce, are not referred. Civil divorces of non-Muslims under the 2022 law go straight to court (Article 3). See our guide to the <a href="/en/family-guidance-section-uae/">Family Guidance section</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Language.</strong> Hearings and filings are in Arabic, so foreign documents need a legal translation. We have found no announcement of an English-language civil family court in Sharjah like the one Abu Dhabi runs.</li>
          <li><strong>Appeals.</strong> You have 30 days to appeal a personal status judgment (Article 9 of the 2024 law), counted from the day after the judgment if you attended, or from the day after you were notified if you did not (Article 10). Appeals go to the Court of Appeal and, on points of law, to the Sharjah Court of Cassation.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Two legal tracks: Muslim and non-Muslim</h2>
        <p>
          Which law governs your divorce in Sharjah depends on the religion and nationality of the spouses, not on where you live inside the emirate.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 on Personal Status</strong>, in force since 15 April 2025, replaced the older Law No. 28 of 2005 across the UAE, Sharjah included. It applies to UAE citizens where both spouses or one of them is Muslim, and to foreign couples unless one spouse insists on applying his or her own law (Article 1). Under it the judge may refer a divorce case to Family Guidance (Article 8; referral was mandatory under the 2005 law), with no fixed statutory period for that stage; in contested harm cases the arbitrators appointed by the court have up to 60 days from their appointment (Article 72; it was 90). Custody of children continues to the age of 18 for boys and girls (Article 123), and a child aged 15 or older can choose which parent to live with unless the child's interest requires otherwise (Article 122).
        </p>
        <p>
          Non-Muslim expatriates can rely on <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 on Civil Personal Status</strong>, the UAE's civil family law, unless one spouse adheres to the law of their home country (Article 1). Either spouse can end the marriage without proving harm (Articles 7 and 8), custody is a joint and equal right of both parents (Article 10), and the court decides alimony on application after the divorce (Article 9). There is no automatic 50/50 split of assets. In Sharjah this civil track is heard by the ordinary Sharjah Courts in Arabic rather than a separate English-language civil family court, which is the main practical difference from Abu Dhabi. Our <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">non-Muslim divorce guide</a> covers the civil track in detail.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Sharjah vs. Dubai Divorce Courts, Key Differences</h2>
        <div class="compare-table">
          <div class="compare-header">
            <div>Factor</div>
            <div>Sharjah</div>
            <div>Dubai</div>
          </div>
          {differences.map((row, i) => (
            <div class={`compare-row${i % 2 === 1 ? ' alt' : ''}`}>
              <div class="compare-factor">{row.factor}</div>
              <div class="compare-sharjah">{row.sharjah}</div>
              <div class="compare-dubai">{row.dubai}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How Sharjah differs from Dubai and Abu Dhabi</h2>
        <p>
          For Muslim couples all three emirates apply the same federal law, Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, so the rules on paper are identical. The differences are the court system, the venue for non-Muslims and local practice.
        </p>
        <p>
          All three now run their own local judiciaries: Abu Dhabi through the Abu Dhabi Judicial Department, Dubai through the Dubai Courts, and Sharjah through its Judicial Department since 2025. Abu Dhabi went furthest for non-Muslims: under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 it runs a <a href="/en/family-court-abu-dhabi/">Civil Family Court</a> with bilingual Arabic and English forms and proceedings. Dubai hears non-Muslim divorces in the Dubai Courts. The English-language DIFC Courts are sometimes described as an alternative, but they have no family jurisdiction and cannot grant a divorce. Sharjah has no separate civil family court for non-Muslims. Non-Muslim residents in Sharjah still use the federal civil law, but before the ordinary Sharjah Courts and in Arabic, which makes a competent local lawyer and translator more important.
        </p>
        <p>
          Local practice also differs from court to court, for example in how often judges refer Muslim cases to Family Guidance and how many sessions a contested case needs. None of this changes your legal rights. Where you can file is decided by residence, work and the marital home (Article 3 of the 2024 law), not by preference, so if you are weighing Sharjah against Dubai, read our <a href="/en/family-court-dubai/">Dubai family court guide</a> and check where you can actually file.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The divorce process in Sharjah, step by step</h2>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li><strong>Filing the claim.</strong> You or your lawyer register the case with the Sharjah Courts, online through the Sharjah Judiciary Platform or in person, pay the fee shown at registration and submit the translated, attested documents. Under the Civil Procedure Code the fee must be paid within 3 business days of the payment notice or the registration is void (Article 47).</li>
          <li><strong>Family Guidance, if the judge refers you.</strong> In a Muslim case the supervising judge may refer the spouses to Family Guidance, where a counsellor tries to help them settle. A settlement is recorded, approved by the judge and enforceable; if there is none, the case goes back to court (Article 8 of the 2024 law). Civil divorces of non-Muslims are not referred.</li>
          <li><strong>Service and first hearing.</strong> The other spouse is formally served, which can be by recorded call, SMS, email or smart applications as well as by hand (Article 9 of the Civil Procedure Code), and the first hearing is scheduled. Uncontested cases where both sides sign an agreement can be concluded quickly.</li>
          <li><strong>Response and evidence.</strong> In a contested case the respondent files a defence, normally within 10 business days of service (Article 48 of the Civil Procedure Code). The court may hear the case over several sessions and appoint experts, for example on finances, or arbitrators in a harm case (up to 60 days from appointment, Article 72 of the 2024 law).</li>
          <li><strong>Judgment.</strong> The judge rules on the divorce and on connected claims such as alimony (nafaqa), the deferred dowry, custody and child maintenance.</li>
          <li><strong>Appeal window.</strong> Either party can appeal within 30 days (Article 9 of the 2024 law). Our step-by-step <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">filing guide for Dubai courts</a> mirrors most of these stages if you want a second worked example.</li>
          <li><strong>Using the judgment abroad.</strong> Once final, obtain a certified copy; for use abroad it is attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs and legalised by the embassy of the country where you will use it. See our <a href="/en/divorce-certificate-attestation-uae/">divorce certificate attestation guide</a>.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Reconciliation in Sharjah: when Family Guidance applies</h2>
        <p>
          Family Guidance applies only if the judge refers a Muslim case to it. Under Article 8 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 the supervising judge may refer the spouses for an amicable settlement if he or she thinks it feasible; under the 2005 law this was mandatory. The law sets no fixed period for the stage. Urgent and temporary orders on alimony, custody and guardianship are exempt, and so are cases where reconciliation is not foreseeable, such as cases to prove a marriage or a divorce. If you settle, the agreement is signed by both spouses and the counsellor, approved by the judge and has the force of an execution writ. In contested harm cases a separate 60-day limit applies to the arbitrators the court appoints, counted from their appointment (Article 72). Civil divorces of non-Muslims under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 are not referred at all (Article 3). Going in with a clear, documented position, and a lawyer who can present it in Arabic, helps either way.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Documents you need and where to file</h2>
        <p>Prepare these before you approach the court:</p>
        <ul class="doc-list">
          <li>Original and translated marriage certificate; if issued abroad, certified in the issuing country, legalised by the UAE embassy there and attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs (the UAE does not accept an apostille on its own)</li>
          <li>Emirates ID and passport copies, with residence visa pages, for both spouses</li>
          <li>Birth certificates for any children, translated and attested</li>
          <li>Proof of Sharjah residence, such as a tenancy contract or utility bill</li>
          <li>For financial claims: salary certificates, bank statements, property title deeds and similar evidence, each translated</li>
          <li>Any prenuptial or postnuptial agreement, if one exists</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Filing is done with the Sharjah Courts, either by your lawyer, through a typing centre, or online through the Sharjah Judiciary Platform, which covers personal status cases. Non-Arabic documents should be translated by a licensed legal translator first; an uncertified translation risks being rejected. If you live abroad, the case must name an elected domicile and a legal representative in the UAE (Article 44 of the Civil Procedure Code).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What to Look for in a Sharjah Divorce Lawyer</h2>
        <ul class="checklist">
          {lawyerChecklist.map(item => (
            <li>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>When to hire a Sharjah-based family lawyer</h2>
        <p>
          You are not legally required to use a lawyer, but a few situations make one close to essential in Sharjah. Hire representation early if your case is contested, if children and custody are involved, if there are assets in more than one country, or if you and your spouse follow different personal status tracks, one Muslim and one non-Muslim. Only lawyers licensed in the UAE can represent you in court (Article 6 of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022), and a lawyer who regularly appears in the Sharjah Courts will know local practice and the new court structure, which a Dubai-only firm may not. You can also authorise your spouse, an in-law or a blood relative up to the fourth degree to appear for you (Article 8 of the same law).
        </p>
        <p>
          For a simple mutual-consent divorce with no children you may only need a lawyer to draft and file the agreement. For anything contested, engage one before the first reconciliation session, not after, because positions taken early shape the rest of the case. Our guide to <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-fees-uae/">divorce lawyer fees in the UAE</a> explains how to compare quotes so you are not overcharged.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Divorce Lawyer Fees in Sharjah</h2>
        <p>
          There is no official tariff for lawyers' fees. The ranges below are indicative market figures only; what you pay depends on the firm, the number of hearings, experts and whether there is an appeal.
        </p>
        <div class="fee-grid">
          <div class="fee-card">
            <p class="fee-type">Uncontested / Mutual Consent</p>
            <p class="fee-amount">AED 3,000 to 8,000</p>
            <p class="fee-detail">Both spouses agree on all terms. Lawyer prepares and files the agreement.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="fee-card">
            <p class="fee-type">Contested (no children)</p>
            <p class="fee-amount">AED 8,000 to 25,000</p>
            <p class="fee-detail">Asset disputes, alimony disagreements. Multiple hearings likely.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="fee-card">
            <p class="fee-type">Contested with custody</p>
            <p class="fee-amount">AED 20,000 to 50,000+</p>
            <p class="fee-detail">Custody cases are the most expensive and time-intensive.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="fee-card">
            <p class="fee-type">Khula (wife-initiated)</p>
            <p class="fee-amount">AED 5,000 to 15,000</p>
            <p class="fee-detail">Depends on whether the husband agrees the consideration or the court has to order khula (Article 66).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="cost-note">
          Beyond the lawyer's fee, budget for court and administrative costs: the court fee, which is shown when you register the case and must be paid within 3 business days of the payment notice (Article 47 of the Civil Procedure Code), legal translation and attestation of foreign documents, which depend on how many pages you have, and the fees of any court-appointed expert in a contested financial or custody matter. Ask for a written estimate that lists each of these separately. For a full breakdown across the country, see our <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">UAE divorce cost guide</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

